window.SKIM_STORY = window.SKIM_STORY || {};
window.SKIM_STORY["gpt-1"] = Object.assign(window.SKIM_STORY["gpt-1"] || {}, {"n-the-mismatch": {"id": "n-the-mismatch", "parentId": "root", "parentChapterId": "the-mismatch", "depth": 1, "title": "The Mismatch, and the Claim", "intro": "The parent gave the problem and the promise. This level reads the introduction as an argument: two separate reasons for wanting raw text, two questions the paper says were blocking anyone from using it, the preconditions the recipe quietly assumes, the single sentence offered in defence of the architecture, and what the headline claim does and does not cover.", "chapters": [{"id": "two-arguments", "title": "Two Arguments, Not One", "body": "The parent gave one reason to want raw text. The introduction gives two, and they are not the same reason.\n\nThe first is about coverage. Most deep learning methods need substantial hand-labelled data, and that requirement rules them out entirely in domains where annotation is thin ([[labeled-data-scarcity]]). The argument here is that supervised learning is unavailable, not that it is weak.\n\nThe second argument is a different shape. The paper says that even where considerable supervision is available, learning good representations without labels still gives a real gain.\n\nScarcity plays no part in that claim. It says unsupervised learning finds structure that training on a task's own labels would not find, however many labels you had ([[generative-vs-discriminative-training]]).\n\nThat second claim needs evidence, and the introduction names some. Pre-trained word embeddings are already in wide use across [[natural-language-understanding|language understanding tasks]], and the paper calls this the most compelling evidence so far that unsupervised representations help ([[word-embedding-transfer]]).\n\nThen it names the limit of that evidence. It is word-level. One vector per word type, learned once, reused everywhere.\n\nThe stated difficulty is getting above that line — using more than word-level information from unlabelled text ([[phrase-and-sentence-embeddings]]).\n\nSo the question the paper opens on is not whether unlabelled text helps at all. Inside [[semi-supervised-learning-for-nlp|semi-supervised NLP]] that is treated as already answered at the word level. What is open is whether anything larger than a word transfers.", "childId": null, "number": "1.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 8]}}, {"id": "two-questions", "title": "What the Two Open Questions Were Made Of", "body": "The parent said two things stood in the way. The introduction says what each one consisted of.\n\nThe first is the objective ([[choice-of-pretraining-objective]]). The problem was not a shortage of candidates. The paper names three recent lines of work: predicting the next token ([[language-modeling-objective|language modelling]]), translating between languages, and judging discourse coherence.\n\nEach one, the paper says, outperforms the others on different tasks. That is the actual state of the evidence — not a ranking, a scatter.\n\nThe footnote attached to that sentence is not a citation. It is a link to a public leaderboard ([[glue-benchmark]]), which is a fair signal of how live the question still was.\n\nNote what the introduction does not do. It picks language modelling and offers no argument here for the choice. The argument is deferred to measurements later in the paper.\n\nThe second question is transfer ([[how-to-transfer-representations]]). Suppose you already hold a pre-trained representation. Existing practice, as the paper describes it, mixed three kinds of machinery: changes to the model architecture shaped around the target task ([[task-specific-architectures]]), intricate learning schemes, and extra unsupervised loss terms carried alongside the supervised one ([[auxiliary-training-objectives]]). A further option was to freeze the pre-trained model and pipe its hidden states into a separate task network as features ([[feature-based-transfer]]).\n\nSo \"no consensus\" means something specific. Several mechanisms were in use at once, each traced to a different paper, with no agreement about which was carrying the result.\n\nThe paper's own diagnosis closes the paragraph. These two uncertainties are why effective semi-supervised approaches for language had been difficult to build.", "childId": "n-the-mismatch--two-questions", "number": "1.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]}}, {"id": "what-it-assumes", "title": "What the Recipe Assumes It Has", "body": "The [[two-stage-training-procedure|two-stage procedure]] has preconditions, and the introduction states them before describing the method.\n\nOne: access to a large corpus of unlabelled text — here, a collection of books ([[bookscorpus]]). Two: several datasets of manually annotated training examples, which the paper calls the target tasks ([[labeled-dataset-c]]).\n\nThen comes one sentence that is easy to read past. The setup does not require the target tasks to be in the same domain as the unlabelled corpus ([[domain-mismatch-tolerance]]).\n\nIt is worth sitting with that. If domain match were required, every target task would need its own matched pile of raw text.\n\nThe scarcity problem would then return one level up, in a different currency — unlabelled text is abundant in general, but not necessarily abundant and on-topic. The point of pre-training once is that the same weights ([[transfer-learning]]) serve tasks whose text looks nothing like the corpus.\n\nThe paper asserts this rather than testing it directly. The evidence is indirect: one corpus of books, then [[evaluation-task-families|twelve datasets]] drawn from image captions, news wire, Wikipedia, science exams and a question forum.\n\nThe goal is stated as a universal representation that transfers with little adaptation. Two separate commitments are packed into that phrase.\n\nUniversal means one representation for all the tasks, rather than one per task ([[task-agnostic-model]]).\n\nLittle adaptation means the cost of taking on a new task should be small. Later in the paper that becomes a countable quantity: how many freshly initialized parameters each new task adds ([[minimal-extra-parameters]]).", "childId": null, "number": "1.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "architecture-reason", "title": "The One Sentence on the Architecture", "body": "The parent said the network is prior work's transformer, not a new design. The introduction does give a reason for that choice, and the reason is one sentence long.\n\nThe [[transformer-gpt1|transformer]] is said to provide a more structured memory for handling long-term dependencies in text, compared with alternatives like recurrent networks ([[lstm-gpt1]]).\n\nUnpack that. A recurrent network carries everything it has read in a single hidden state of fixed size, rewritten at every step. Older material survives only to the extent that it survived each rewrite.\n\nSelf-attention instead keeps every earlier position addressable. A prediction can reach back to a specific token directly, without that token having to have been preserved through hundreds of updates.\n\nWhat the paper wants from that reach is [[long-range-dependencies-gpt1|dependencies spanning many tokens]] — a pronoun and the name it stands for several sentences earlier, or an exam question and the one line of the passage that answers it.\n\nThen the introduction takes a further step. It says this structured memory results in robust transfer performance across diverse tasks ([[transformer-inductive-bias]]).\n\nAt this point in the paper that is an assertion. The citations offered are for the transformer's strength on machine translation, document generation and syntactic parsing. None of those are transfer results.\n\nThe evidence comes much later, and as a removal: hold the whole recipe fixed, swap in a single-layer LSTM, and the average score falls by 5.6 points ([[ablation-lstm-substitution]]).\n\nThe same paragraph also commits early to handling [[structured-inputs|multi-part inputs]] by walking them into one contiguous sequence ([[traversal-style-approach]]), rather than by building task-shaped modules ([[task-specific-input-transformations]]).", "childId": null, "number": "1.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "the-claim", "title": "The Claim, and What It Leaves Out", "body": "The parent gave the headline: new best published results on [[state-of-the-art-9-of-12|nine of twelve datasets]]. Read the sentence closely and three things sharpen.\n\nFirst, the comparison class. The model is said to outperform discriminatively trained models that use architectures specifically crafted for each task ([[baseline-comparisons]]). The claim is staked against hand-built task systems. It is not a claim about beating other pre-trained models.\n\nSecond, the spread. Four families are named up front: [[natural-language-inference|inference]], [[question-answering-and-commonsense-reasoning|question answering]], [[semantic-similarity|semantic similarity]] and [[text-classification|text classification]], covering [[table-1|twelve datasets]] between them. Breadth is part of the claim itself, since a single model that only worked on one family would settle nothing.\n\nThird, how gains are counted. The introduction reports two figures the parent had no room for: 1.5 points on [[multinli|MultiNLI]] and 5.5 on the GLUE multi-task benchmark, beside 8.9 on the [[story-cloze-test|Story Cloze Test]] and 5.7 on [[race-dataset|RACE]].\n\nThese are absolute improvements ([[absolute-improvement]]). A gain of 8.9 means the accuracy figure moved 8.9 percentage points.\n\nIt does not mean errors fell by 8.9 percent. That relative figure would be the larger-sounding one on any task already scoring well, and the paper does not use it.\n\nNow what the claim leaves out. Nine of twelve means three datasets where the previous best still stands, and the introduction does not name them.\n\nOne further piece of evidence is promised here. The paper says it also analysed [[zero-shot-behaviors|zero-shot behaviours]] across four settings, offered as a sign that pre-training alone leaves useful linguistic knowledge behind before any label is seen.", "childId": null, "number": "1.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-which-family-this-joins": {"id": "n-which-family-this-joins", "parentId": "root", "parentChapterId": "which-family-this-joins", "depth": 1, "title": "Which Family This Joins", "intro": "The parent named the branches. This level says what each branch actually does to a model — which parameters move, which stay frozen, which loss terms are summed — and checks how much of the paper's own placement is argument and how much is measurement.", "chapters": [{"id": "what-a-word-table-carries", "title": "What a Word Table Can Carry", "body": "The oldest members of [[semi-supervised-learning-for-nlp]] learned nothing at all. They computed word-level and phrase-level statistics over the unlabelled corpus — counts, co-occurrence figures — and handed those to a supervised model as extra input features.\n\nLearned vectors came later. [[word-embedding-transfer]] trains one vector per vocabulary entry on raw text, then drops that table into a task model.\n\nThe mechanism is a lookup. Each word type owns one row. The row returned for a word is the same row every time that word appears, whatever sentence it appears in.\n\nSo the unlabelled corpus contributes a fixed quantity per word, settled before the task model sees anything. The tasks this was shown on match that granularity: sequence labelling, and [[text-classification]].\n\n[[phrase-and-sentence-embeddings]] push the unit upward. One vector now stands for a whole phrase or sentence, trained on unlabelled text, then used as a feature for the target task. The vector is longer-range, but it is still a frozen quantity passed to a separately built network.\n\nThe paper's own model contains a table of exactly the first kind. [[token-embedding-matrix]] $W_e$ maps each token to a dense vector, and [[input-representation-h0]] adds position vectors to it: $h_0 = UW_e + W_p$.\n\nThat is layer zero. Twelve blocks sit above it, and [[final-token-activation]] at any position depends on every preceding token in the window.\n\nSo the thing carried across is not the table. It is the stack that reads the table — which is what lets a [[natural-language-understanding]] task see [[long-range-dependencies-gpt1]] rather than a bag of per-word constants.", "childId": null, "number": "2.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "loss-or-starting-point", "title": "Change the Loss, or Change the Starting Point", "body": "The second cut is about which part of training the unlabelled data touches.\n\n[[auxiliary-training-objectives]] touch the loss. There is one training run, and the objective is a sum: the supervised term plus an unsupervised term computed on raw text. Gradients from both flow into the same shared weights at the same time.\n\nThe cited instances are concrete. Collobert and Weston attached a spread of auxiliary NLP tasks — part-of-speech tagging, chunking, named entity recognition, language modelling — to improve semantic role labelling. Rei added a single language modelling term beside a sequence labelling objective and reported gains.\n\n[[unsupervised-pretraining]] leaves the objective alone. The supervised loss is exactly what it would have been; only the initial value of the weights differs.\n\nThat is a clean distinction, and the paper does not sit cleanly on one side of it.\n\n[[supervised-fine-tuning]] here keeps a language modelling term running during stage two. [[auxiliary-lm-objective]] is written as $L_3(C) = L_2(C) + \\lambda\\, L_1(C)$, and [[eq-combined-objective]] is the paper's Equation 5. [[auxiliary-objective-weight]] is fixed at $\\lambda = 0.5$.\n\nBy the branch definition, the paper is doing both things.\n\nIts defence is a claim about magnitude, not category: pre-training already learns the linguistic aspects the auxiliary term would supply, so the term is a top-up rather than the engine.\n\n[[ablation-no-auxiliary-objective]] is where that claim is cashed. In [[table-5]] the auxiliary term helps the larger datasets and hurts the smaller ones, and its effect is by far the smallest of the three removals tested.", "childId": null, "number": "2.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "borrowed-technique", "title": "A Technique Borrowed From Elsewhere", "body": "The branch the paper joins did not start in NLP.\n\nThe early citations are image classification and regression. Unsupervised pre-training there was a way to get a deep network to train at all, by putting the weights somewhere reasonable before the supervised gradient arrived.\n\nThen came a sharper finding. [[pretraining-as-regularization]] reports that the benefit is not only that optimization gets easier — the initialization also lands the network somewhere that generalizes better on held-out data.\n\nThat matters for how the paper reads its own results. If pre-training were only an optimization aid, its value would shrink as labelled data grew and optimization stopped being the binding constraint. The regularization reading says otherwise.\n\nThe paper cites this result. It does not re-run it.\n\nMore recent applications named are image classification again, speech recognition, entity disambiguation and machine translation. So the technique arrives already established across domains, and what is specific here is the domain and the choice of objective.\n\nOne consequence is stated as an assumption rather than tested directly. [[domain-mismatch-tolerance]] holds that the unlabelled corpus need not match the target domain, which is what permits books to initialize models scored on news sentences, Quora questions and science exams.\n\nThe paper's own measurement of what initialization is worth comes from [[ablation-no-pretraining]] in [[ablation-studies]]: the same transformer, the same [[two-stage-training-procedure]] minus stage one, trained straight on the supervised tasks, loses 14.8% across the board.\n\nThat number isolates [[transfer-learning]] cleanly, because architecture and fine-tuning objective are held fixed and only [[generative-pretraining]] is removed.", "childId": null, "number": "2.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "freeze-or-carry", "title": "Freeze the Model, or Carry Its Weights", "body": "Inside the initialization branch, the split is over what happens to the pre-trained weights once the target task arrives.\n\n[[feature-based-transfer]] keeps them still. A pre-trained language or translation model is run over the task input, hidden states are read out of it, and those states are fed to a separate supervised network as extra features. No gradient from the task updates the pre-trained model.\n\n[[elmo-deep-contextualized-word-representations]] is the instance named. Its features come from a pre-trained bidirectional language model, and they are consumed by a task network that is still designed and initialized per task.\n\nThat network is the cost. [[per-task-parameter-cost]] is the paper's objection: each new target task brings a substantial set of freshly initialized parameters, trained from scratch on that task's small labelled set.\n\nThe paper gives no count for this. The argument stays qualitative on the other side.\n\nOn its own side the arithmetic is short. [[minimal-extra-parameters]] amounts to [[linear-output-layer-wy]], a matrix from a 768-dimensional activation to however many classes the task has, plus embedding rows for the [[delimiter-token]] and the bracketing symbols. For a three-way inference task that is a few thousand new weights against a twelve-layer stack inherited whole.\n\nEverything else is [[task-agnostic-model]]: one architecture, pre-trained once, not redesigned for anything — against [[task-specific-architectures]] built around each task's shape.\n\nThe divergence reaches the data as well. [[one-billion-word-benchmark]], the corpus behind the feature-based comparison, is about the same size as [[bookscorpus]] but shuffled at the sentence level, which removes the [[long-range-contiguous-text]] this paper says its corpus was chosen for.", "childId": null, "number": "2.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "closest-line", "title": "The Closest Line, and What It Is Missing", "body": "[[lm-pretraining-then-finetuning-predecessors]] is the line the paper calls closest to its own, and the closeness is real: pre-train with a language modelling objective, then fine-tune the whole network with supervision.\n\n[[semi-supervised-sequence-learning]] did this with a recurrent network, pre-trained by language modelling or by autoencoding, then fine-tuned for [[text-classification]]. [[ulmfit]] pre-trained an LSTM language model and fine-tuned it on a staged schedule, again for classification.\n\nThe recipe is therefore not what is being claimed.\n\nTwo differences are claimed instead. [[lstm-short-range-limitation]] is the first: the paper argues a recurrent pre-trained model restricts its prediction ability to a short range, so however much linguistic information the pre-training phase captures, only nearby structure survives the transfer.\n\nIn the related work section this is asserted, with a forward pointer to the experiments. Nothing is measured there.\n\nWhat arrives later is indirect but real. [[ablation-lstm-substitution]] swaps in a single-layer 2048-unit LSTM inside the same framework and loses 5.6 average points, winning on exactly one dataset, [[mrpc]]. [[layers-transferred]] in [[fig-2]] shows each transferred block adding accuracy, up to 9% on MultiNLI. [[transformer-inductive-bias]] rests on the LSTM showing higher variance in zero-shot scores.\n\nNone of that measures range directly. Range is inferred from an averaged score gap, supported by a [[context-window-512-tokens]] over contiguous book text.\n\nThe second difference needs no inference. The predecessors were evaluated on text classification; [[evaluation-task-families]] here spans inference, paraphrase detection and story completion across the twelve datasets of [[table-1]].", "childId": "n-which-family-this-joins--closest-line", "number": "2.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-one-set-of-weights-used-twice": {"id": "n-one-set-of-weights-used-twice", "parentId": "root", "parentChapterId": "one-set-of-weights-used-twice", "depth": 1, "title": "One Set of Weights, Used Twice", "intro": "The parent gave the recipe's shape. This level takes it apart at the level of parameters: what actually crosses from stage one into stage two, what is added, what fine-tuning everything costs, where the per-task work went once it left the architecture, and how much weight the domain assumption is carrying.", "chapters": [{"id": "what-crosses", "title": "What Crosses the Boundary", "body": "Fine-tuning here begins by loading a file of numbers. What sits in that file is the entire product of [[generative-pretraining|stage one]]: the [[token-embedding-matrix]], the position embeddings, and every weight in every block of the stack.\n\n[[supervised-fine-tuning|Stage two]] does not wrap that file in anything. It sets the network's parameters to those values and carries on running gradient descent, now against a different loss. That is the whole of [[transfer-learning|the transfer]] in this [[two-stage-training-procedure|two-stage recipe]].\n\nWhich makes the interesting question an accounting one. How many parameters does a new task need that stage one did not already supply?\n\nThe paper's answer is two kinds, and it states them as a total ([[minimal-extra-parameters]]).\n\nThe first is the output layer [[linear-output-layer-wy|$W_y$]]. It maps the 768-dimensional activation at the last token to one score per label, so for a three-way entailment decision it is $768 \\times 3$ numbers ([[eq-task-head]]).\n\nThe second is an embedding for each [[delimiter-token|delimiter symbol]] the rewritten inputs introduce, because those symbols carry no meaning learned during pre-training. Everything else is inherited.\n\nSet that against the alternative the paper is arguing with. In [[feature-based-transfer]], the pre-trained model is frozen and run only to produce hidden states, which are handed to a separate supervised network as extra input features; [[elmo-deep-contextualized-word-representations|ELMo]] is the best-known instance. That separate network is designed and initialized for each task, so its full parameter count is a per-task cost.\n\nThe paper turns this into its comparison metric, [[per-task-parameter-cost]], and says such approaches involve a substantial number of new parameters for each separate target task.\n\nNote what form that comparison takes. It is asserted, not counted: no parameter totals for either side appear anywhere in the paper.", "childId": null, "number": "3.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "nothing-frozen", "title": "The Price of Not Freezing", "body": "If no layer is frozen, every weight in the twelve blocks is a free variable while the task loss is being minimized. Two consequences follow that the paper does not dwell on.\n\nThe first is bookkeeping. Fine-tuning ends with a full, separately modified copy of the [[model-specification|12-layer, 768-dimensional stack]] for each dataset, so twelve datasets means twelve models on disk.\n\nA frozen pre-trained model with small task heads would keep one copy and add little per task. The paper's accounting counts freshly initialized parameters, where it wins; the storage accounting runs the other way, and is not reported.\n\nThe second is that gradients from a small labelled set can move those weights anywhere, including away from whatever made them worth transferring. Nothing in the framework prevents that.\n\nThe paper does not measure it either. It cites the older finding that [[unsupervised-pretraining|unsupervised pre-training]] acts as [[pretraining-as-regularization|a regularizer]] rather than only an optimization aid, which is a claim that the starting point keeps mattering — but that is a citation, not a measurement made here.\n\nTwo of its own choices bear on the risk without being presented as remedies for it. Fine-tuning runs at a learning rate well below the pre-training peak, for three passes over the task data ([[fine-tuning-hyperparameters]]). And the language modelling loss keeps being optimized during stage two ([[auxiliary-lm-objective]]), which the paper justifies by generalization and convergence speed, not by drift.\n\nWhat the paper does supply is evidence that carrying the weights across is worth doing at all: one row of [[table-5]] retrains the same architecture with [[ablation-no-pretraining|no pre-training]], and a sweep in [[layers-transferred]] carries only part of the stack. Both live in a later chapter.", "childId": null, "number": "3.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "where-the-task-work-went", "title": "Where the Task-Specific Work Went", "body": "The tasks in this evaluation are not the same shape. A sentiment example is one sentence; an entailment example is an ordered pair; a reading comprehension example is a document, a question and several candidate answers.\n\nSomething has to absorb that difference. It does not vanish because a model is called general.\n\nIn the systems the paper measures against, the architecture absorbs it. Those are [[task-specific-architectures]]: [[baseline-comparisons|mostly biLSTM]] networks with attention arranged around one task's structure, sometimes ensembled, which is what most published numbers on these datasets came from at the time.\n\nHere the input absorbs it. [[structured-inputs]] are walked into a single ordered token sequence, a [[traversal-style-approach]], so the [[multi-layer-transformer-decoder|same stack]] can read them unchanged ([[fig-1]], [[task-specific-input-transformations]]).\n\nSo [[task-agnostic-model|task-agnostic]] is a claim about the network, not about the pipeline. Per-task work still exists. It has moved from choosing layers to choosing a token order and a set of separator symbols.\n\nThe abstract is more careful than the shorthand suggests: it says task-aware input transformations, used to achieve transfer while requiring minimal changes to the model architecture.\n\nAnd for two task families the per-task decision reaches slightly past the token string. [[similarity-input-transformation|Similarity]] runs both sentence orderings through the stack and adds the two final activations. [[qa-and-commonsense-input-transformation|Multiple choice]] runs one sequence per candidate answer and normalizes the scores across them.\n\nThose are choices about how many forward passes to make and how to combine them. The stack itself is untouched, but the claim of no task-specific structure is doing slightly less work than it first appears.", "childId": null, "number": "3.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "books-to-exams", "title": "Books In, Science Exams Out", "body": "The [[domain-mismatch-tolerance|domain assumption]] appears once, in the introduction, as a single sentence about the setup: the target tasks are not required to be in the same domain as the unlabelled corpus.\n\nIt is worth seeing how wide the gap actually is. [[bookscorpus]] is over 7,000 unpublished books, in genres the paper lists as adventure, fantasy and romance.\n\nNow the other end. [[snli]] is written over image captions. [[qnli]] is built from Wikipedia. [[scitail]] comes from science exam questions. [[rte]] and [[mrpc]] come from news. [[race-dataset]] is English exam passages set for Chinese students.\n\nNone of those reads like a work of fiction. The assumption is carrying every one of these datasets.\n\nWhat would test it is a second pre-training run on a matched corpus, scored the same way. No such run is reported, so the assumption stays an assumption.\n\nThe corpus argument the paper does make runs on a different axis entirely. It names the [[one-billion-word-benchmark]] as the rejected alternative and argues from [[long-range-contiguous-text|contiguity]], not from subject matter — and even that comparison is made by argument rather than by training on both.\n\nThere is weak support in the results. The same weights do well across captions, news, Wikipedia and exam text, which is at least consistent with domain mattering little, though nothing here isolates domain as the variable.\n\nOne case is worth flagging. RTE is the single inference dataset where the paper does not take the best published number, and it reads that as a small-data effect at 2,490 training examples. RTE is also news text. The paper's reading may be right, but a domain reading is available and is not ruled out.\n\nThe conclusion goes further still, claiming the books leave the model with [[world-knowledge|world knowledge]] that transfers. That is an interpretation of the gains, not a separate measurement.", "childId": null, "number": "3.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-predicting-the-next-token": {"id": "n-predicting-the-next-token", "parentId": "root", "parentChapterId": "predicting-the-next-token", "depth": 1, "title": "Predicting the Next Token", "intro": "The parent gave the loss and named the network. This level reads both at working scale: what one 512-token sequence actually buys in scored predictions, what the mask does inside a single attention head, what each line of Equation 2 commits the model to, and how large the stack that results is.", "chapters": [{"id": "one-sum-many-terms", "title": "Reading the Sum as Work Done", "body": "$k$ is a number, and the paper fixes it. [[eq-lm-objective|Equation 1]] writes the context as $u_{i-k},\\dots,u_{i-1}$, and the trained model reads contiguous sequences of 512 tokens ([[context-window-512-tokens]]). A prediction can therefore condition on up to 511 tokens of preceding text.\n\nThat is paragraphs, not words. A pronoun and the name it stands for can sit several sentences apart, and a window this wide holds both at once ([[long-range-dependencies-gpt1]]).\n\nNow read the sum as work rather than as notation. Take one sequence of 512 tokens. Every position after the first contributes a term to $L_1$, so a single sequence yields on the order of 511 scored predictions.\n\nEach token in it plays two roles. Once it is the target, at its own position. Up to 511 times it is context, for the positions that come after it.\n\nNothing was annotated to arrange this. The target at position $i$ is the token already sitting at position $i$, so the label is the data shifted by one ([[generative-pretraining]]).\n\nThe terms are log-probabilities, so each is at most zero. A model that puts probability $0.5$ on the token that appeared contributes about $-0.69$; at $0.05$ it contributes about $-3.0$; at $0.9$, about $-0.11$. Driving the sum up means moving probability mass onto the tokens that actually occurred, and stage one applies no other pressure ([[maximum-likelihood-training]], [[language-modeling-objective]]).\n\nThe paper says these parameters are trained with stochastic gradient descent, and says no more than that here. They are the same parameters the second stage will start from ([[two-stage-training-procedure]]).", "childId": null, "number": "4.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8]}}, {"id": "the-mask", "title": "The Mask, and What the Missing Encoder Costs", "body": "Inside one attention head the model forms a score for every ordered pair of positions: the query at $i$ against the key at $j$. [[masked-self-attention]] intervenes before the softmax over those scores. Every entry with $j > i$ is set to $-\\infty$, so its weight after the softmax is exactly zero.\n\nThe output at position $i$ is then a mixture of values drawn from positions $1$ through $i$ and nothing else.\n\nThat property composes. If each layer is causal, a stack of them is causal, so a state at the top of the blocks still depends on no token after its own ([[multi-layer-transformer-decoder]]).\n\nThis is what makes scoring every position in one pass legitimate. Without the mask you would need a separate forward pass per position, each with the future cut away. With it, one pass over a 512-token sequence produces every term of the sum, and no term has seen its own answer.\n\nThe encoder is dropped for a separate reason. In the architecture this builds on ([[transformer-gpt1]]) a decoder block carries three sublayers, the middle one attending to an encoder's output. Pre-training has no source sequence and no target sequence, only one running stream of text, so that sublayer has nothing to read. Removing it leaves masked self-attention and a position-wise feed-forward layer per block ([[decoder-only-stack]]) — the block drawn on the left of [[fig-1]].\n\nThe cost is standing: no position ever sees its right context, in this stage or in the next one.\n\nMasking buys causality that a recurrent network gets by construction. The paper's own measurement of that trade is the swap to a single-layer LSTM, which costs 5.6 average points ([[ablation-lstm-substitution]]).", "childId": null, "number": "4.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "equation-two-line-by-line", "title": "Equation 2, Line by Line", "body": "[[eq-decoder-stack|Equation 2]] is three lines. Take them in order.\n\nLine one. $U$ is the context written as one-hot rows, so $UW_e$ is a matrix product on paper and a row lookup in practice: token $u$ fetches row $u$ of [[token-embedding-matrix]]. Each row is 768 numbers wide ([[hidden-state-dimension-768]]), and there is one row per entry of the subword vocabulary ([[byte-pair-encoding-gpt1]]).\n\nThen $W_p$ is added. Added, not concatenated — [[input-representation-h0]] hands position and identity the same 768 coordinates to share, rather than reserving a slice of the vector for each.\n\n$W_p$ is a trained table with one row per slot ([[learned-position-embeddings]]). It has as many rows as the window and none beyond; the sinusoids it replaces are defined at any index, and a table is not. The paper states the substitution without reporting a comparison.\n\nLine two is $h_l = \\text{transformer\\_block}(h_{l-1})$, applied $n$ times. Shape in equals shape out, which is what makes the blocks stackable at all, and what later lets the analysis carry over some of them rather than all.\n\nLine three is $P(u) = \\mathrm{softmax}(h_n W_e^{\\mathsf{T}})$. Read the multiplication as inner products: the 768-dimensional state at the top is dotted against every row of the embedding table, giving one score per vocabulary entry ([[output-softmax-tied-embeddings]]).\n\nSo a token scores high exactly when the final state points along that token's own input embedding.\n\nTwo consequences follow. The output layer costs no parameters of its own, saving a matrix of vocabulary size by 768. And every row of $W_e$ takes gradient from two directions: wherever that token appeared as input, and at every position where it stood as a candidate.", "childId": null, "number": "4.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}], "pages": [3, 4, 5]}}, {"id": "stack-dimensions", "title": "The Numbers the Stack Was Built With", "body": "[[model-specification]] pins the stack down: twelve blocks ([[twelve-layer-depth]]), 768-dimensional states, twelve attention heads in each block ([[twelve-attention-heads]]), and a feed-forward sublayer whose inner width is 3072 ([[position-wise-feedforward-3072]]).\n\nTwo of those numbers settle a third the paper never prints. Twelve heads splitting 768 dimensions leaves each head a 64-dimensional subspace to attend in.\n\nThe feed-forward width is really a ratio: 3072 is four times 768. Each block expands its representation fourfold, applies a nonlinearity, then projects back down to 768.\n\nThat nonlinearity is [[gelu]] rather than ReLU. It multiplies each input by the probability that a standard normal draw falls below it, so instead of a hard cut at zero the curve bends through it.\n\nThe size is worth working out, since no parameter count is reported. Attention in one block uses four $768 \\times 768$ projections, about 2.4M weights. The feed-forward sublayer uses $768 \\times 3072$ twice, about 4.7M. That is roughly 7.1M per block, so about 85M across the twelve, with a further 31M or so sitting in the embedding table.\n\nInitialization is $\\mathcal{N}(0, 0.02)$ for every weight ([[weight-initialization]]). The paper's stated reason is that [[layer-normalization-gpt1]] is used throughout the stack, so a simple scheme sufficed. It offers no experiment behind that.\n\nDepth is the one of these dimensions the paper later turns as a dial. [[fig-2]] varies how many of the twelve pre-trained blocks are carried into fine-tuning ([[layers-transferred]]) and reports the score climbing with each one added.", "childId": "n-predicting-the-next-token--stack-dimensions", "number": "4.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "what-crosses-over", "title": "What Stage One Hands Over", "body": "Stage one's output is a distribution over the vocabulary, and that distribution is not what gets used next. What crosses into the second stage is the fitted parameters ([[transfer-learning]]).\n\nAnd it is not a partial handover. Because the output layer is the transposed embedding matrix, there is no separate head to discard — every weight the language model has is a weight the next stage inherits ([[supervised-fine-tuning]]).\n\nThe loss itself is not discarded either. $L_1$ returns during fine-tuning as a second term sitting alongside the task loss ([[auxiliary-lm-objective]]).\n\nWhat the paper wants from all this is stated plainly enough. The objective never names a task, so any task-relevant ability in the weights arrives as a byproduct of predicting text ([[task-agnostic-model]]).\n\nThe conclusion claims that training this way leaves the model with knowledge about the world ([[world-knowledge]]). The analysis section puts a mechanism to it as a hypothesis: the generative model picks up abilities the evaluation tasks need because those abilities improve its language modelling, and the transformer's more structured attentional memory assists transfer compared with a recurrent state ([[transformer-inductive-bias]]).\n\nBoth are hypotheses in the paper's own wording. Nothing in the objective or the architecture establishes them; the measurements that bear on them come later.", "childId": null, "number": "4.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8]}}], "number": "4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}, "n-the-corpus-and-the-training-run": {"id": "n-the-corpus-and-the-training-run", "parentId": "root", "parentChapterId": "the-corpus-and-the-training-run", "depth": 1, "title": "The Corpus, the Model, the Run", "intro": "The parent listed the choices. This level shows the mechanisms behind them: what sentence-level shuffling actually removes and what the paper never tested, how raw book text becomes 40,000-merge subword units, how the dimensions of one block fit together, and what a 100-epoch run costs in updates. It ends where the parent ended, on a single perplexity number, and says what that number does and does not establish.", "chapters": [{"id": "corpus-as-argument", "title": "The Corpus as an Argument", "body": "The case for [[bookscorpus]] is comparative, and it repays separating what the comparison establishes from what it assumes.\n\nBoth corpora are about the same size. Size is therefore held fixed, and one property differs: order. Shuffling the [[one-billion-word-benchmark|1B Word Benchmark]] at the sentence level leaves every unigram, every bigram and every within-sentence dependency exactly where it was. What it removes is any statistical relation between one sentence and the next one.\n\nName those relations concretely. A pronoun and the name it stands for, several sentences back, is [[coreference]]. A character introduced in one paragraph and referred to by role in the next is another. These are the [[long-range-dependencies-gpt1|far-apart relations]] that [[language-modeling-objective|the objective]] can only learn if the training text contains them at all.\n\nNow the sampling. Each training sequence is a contiguous run of 512 tokens taken from one book, not 512 tokens assembled from separate pieces. In a shuffled corpus the window would still be full. Most of what filled it would simply be unrelated to the token being predicted. The window is [[context-window-512-tokens|as wide as $k$ allows]] either way; only in [[long-range-contiguous-text|ordered text]] does the extra width carry anything.\n\nThen the part the paper leaves open. It runs no control. It does not pre-train the same model on the shuffled corpus and compare the downstream numbers. Contiguity is given as a reason for a choice, not tested as a hypothesis — [[generative-pretraining|stage one]] was run once, on books.\n\nOne thing the choice does not claim to secure. Books are fiction-heavy; the evaluation tasks include news, science exam questions and Wikipedia. The paper's stated position is [[domain-mismatch-tolerance|that no domain match is required]].", "childId": null, "number": "5.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "raw-text-to-subwords", "title": "Raw Text to Subword Units", "body": "The [[text-preprocessing-pipeline|three steps]] each do a separate job.\n\n[[ftfy]] goes first. Raw book text carries encoding damage: characters mangled by a bad decode, curly and straight quotes mixed together, irregular whitespace. ftfy repairs that and standardizes some punctuation and whitespace. The step matters more than it sounds, because every surface variant the cleaner fails to merge becomes its own set of subword pieces further down.\n\nThe [[spacy-tokenizer|spaCy tokenizer]] then cuts the cleaned text into word-level tokens, splitting punctuation off words and handling contractions.\n\nThen [[byte-pair-encoding-gpt1|byte-pair encoding]]. Start with each word written as its sequence of characters. Count every adjacent symbol pair across the corpus. Take the most frequent pair and record it as a merge: those two symbols are now one symbol. Recount. Merge again. Repeat.\n\nEach merge adds exactly one symbol to the vocabulary. Frequent sequences rise to the top early, so ordinary words become single symbols within the first few thousand merges. Rare words never top the frequency list as wholes, so they stay split into pieces that other words also use.\n\n[[bpe-merges-40000|40,000 merges]] is the entire setting, and it trades two ways. Too few, and almost everything splits toward characters: sequences get longer, and a fixed window spans less text. Too many, and the vocabulary grows while each rare embedding is trained on fewer occurrences.\n\nTwo consequences follow. There is no unknown token, since any string decomposes into pieces already present, down to single characters. And the vocabulary size fixes the shape of the largest matrix in the model: the [[token-embedding-matrix|token embedding matrix $W_e$]], which enters at [[input-representation-h0|$h_0$]] and returns transposed in the [[output-softmax-tied-embeddings|output softmax]].", "childId": null, "number": "5.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}], "pages": [3, 4, 5]}}, {"id": "shape-of-the-stack", "title": "How the Dimensions Fit Together", "body": "The numbers in the [[model-specification|specification]] are not four independent choices.\n\n[[hidden-state-dimension-768|768]] divided by [[twelve-attention-heads|12 heads]] gives each head a 64-dimensional subspace to attend in. The [[position-wise-feedforward-3072|3072-dimensional]] inner layer is $4 \\times 768$: each block expands fourfold and projects back down. Both ratios come straight from the original transformer.\n\nWalk the parameter count, which the paper does not report. Inside one block, the attention sublayer holds four $768 \\times 768$ projections — queries, keys, values, output — at $589{,}824$ each, so about $2.4$M. The feed-forward sublayer holds $768 \\times 3072$ going up and $3072 \\times 768$ coming back, about $4.7$M. That is roughly $7$M per block, and [[twelve-layer-depth|twelve blocks]] of it come to around $85$M. Add roughly $31$M for a 40,000-row embedding table of width 768. The [[decoder-only-stack|single stack]] is therefore on the order of a hundred million parameters.\n\nThree smaller choices are stated, two with reasons and one without.\n\n[[learned-position-embeddings|Position embeddings are learned]] rather than sinusoidal. That makes the window a parameter shape: $W_p$ has exactly 512 rows, one per slot, and nothing is defined beyond them.\n\n[[gelu|GELU]] replaces ReLU. It computes $x\\,\\Phi(x)$, gating each input by the probability that a standard normal draw falls below it — smooth everywhere, and slightly negative for small negative inputs. No ablation is offered for it.\n\nInitialization is a plain $\\mathcal{N}(0, 0.02)$ draw, and the paper says [[weight-initialization|that sufficed]] because [[layer-normalization-gpt1|layer normalization]] is used throughout the stack.\n\nThe depth is also a dial the [[layers-transferred|transfer analysis]] turns later.", "childId": "n-the-corpus-and-the-training-run--shape-of-the-stack", "number": "5.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "the-run", "title": "The Run, Counted in Updates", "body": "The [[pre-training-optimization|optimization setup]] is easier to judge once the schedule is counted in updates rather than epochs.\n\nOne minibatch is 64 sequences of 512 tokens, which is $32{,}768$ tokens per update. The [[linear-warmup|warmup]] runs 2000 updates, so the learning rate climbs from zero to $2.5 \\times 10^{-4}$ over roughly $65$M tokens. If the corpus is on the order of a billion tokens, warmup is a small fraction of the first pass alone. [[pretraining-schedule|100 passes]] over such a corpus works out to a few million updates in total.\n\nWhy warm up at all. [[adam-optimizer-gpt1|Adam]] scales each step by running estimates of the gradient's first and second moments, and those estimates are built from almost no data at the start. Full-size steps taken on them are steps taken on noise.\n\nAfter the peak, [[cosine-annealing|the rate follows a cosine curve]] back to zero across the remaining updates. The curve spends a long stretch near the peak and slows late, and the endpoint is zero rather than a floor.\n\nThe [[regularization-setup|regularizers]] are three plus one. [[dropout-gpt1|Dropout]] at rate 0.1 is applied [[residual-embedding-attention-dropout|on residual branches, at the embedding layer, and inside attention]]. Alongside it runs a [[decoupled-weight-decay|modified L2 penalty]] at $w = 0.01$, applied to every weight that is not a bias or a gain. The exclusion is deliberate: layer normalization's gains and biases exist to rescale activations, and shrinking them toward zero would work against the normalization.\n\nNo compute cost, wall-clock time or sweep over these settings is reported.", "childId": null, "number": "5.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}], "pages": [3, 4, 5]}}, {"id": "one-number", "title": "What 18.4 Establishes", "body": "[[token-level-perplexity|18.4]] is the exponential of the mean negative log-likelihood per token. Loosely, at each position the model is about as uncertain as someone choosing uniformly among eighteen options.\n\nTwo qualifications sit on the number.\n\nThe token is a BPE unit, not a word. Splitting rare words into pieces produces more tokens, and each piece is individually easier to predict than the whole rare word would have been, so a token-level figure is not comparable with a word-level one on the same text.\n\nAnd the paper reports the figure on the corpus, without naming a held-out split. After 100 passes over that corpus, a number stated this way does not separate fit from generalization. It is offered as evidence that [[maximum-likelihood-training|maximizing likelihood]] did what it was asked to do.\n\nThat is the limit of what it carries. A perplexity says nothing about whether the weights help on a labelled task, which is the only thing [[two-stage-training-procedure|the recipe]] is finally judged on. The paper does not argue from it either. Its evidence that pre-training matters comes later, from [[ablation-no-pretraining|the ablation that removes pre-training]] and from [[zero-shot-behaviors|heuristics run on the generative model directly]], not from this line. The claim in the conclusion that the model has picked up [[world-knowledge|knowledge about the world]] rests on those, not on 18.4.\n\nWhat the number does mark is the end of stage one. From here the same weights stop being a language model and start being an initialization for [[supervised-fine-tuning|something else]].", "childId": null, "number": "5.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}], "number": "5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-from-language-model-to-classifier": {"id": "n-from-language-model-to-classifier", "parentId": "root", "parentChapterId": "from-language-model-to-classifier", "depth": 1, "title": "From Language Model to Classifier", "intro": "The summary said what stage two does: read the last token, multiply by one matrix, keep the language modelling loss running, train briefly. This level asks the questions that answer skips — why that position and not another, what the gradient actually touches, what the second loss is computed on, and what the reported settings amount to on a small dataset versus a large one.", "chapters": [{"id": "reading-the-last-position", "title": "Reading the Last Position", "body": "Start with the choice of where to read.\n\nThe stack is causally masked: under [[masked-self-attention]] the activation at position $i$ has attended to positions $1$ through $i$ and nothing after. So the final block's output at position 1 has seen one token. The output at position $m$ has seen all $m$.\n\nOnly the last position is a summary of the whole input. That is why [[eq-task-head]] reads [[final-token-activation]] rather than pooling across positions — under this masking, no other single position has the whole sequence available.\n\nWhat sits on top of it is small. The activation is a 768-dimensional vector ([[hidden-state-dimension-768]]), so [[linear-output-layer-wy]] is a $768 \\times |Y|$ matrix. For a three-way inference task that is 2,304 numbers, added to a stack of twelve blocks ([[twelve-layer-depth]]), each with a 3072-dimensional inner feed-forward layer. The paper never states a total parameter count, but the ratio is not close.\n\nNotice what the head is not. There is no hidden layer, no task-specific attention, no learned pooling. Any nonlinear work a task needs has to already be available inside the pre-trained blocks, because the only thing between them and the label scores is one matrix multiply and a softmax.\n\nThe paper does not argue for this. It writes Equation 3 and lets the scoreboard carry the claim.\n\n[[minimal-extra-parameters]] is the tally: $W_y$, plus embeddings for the [[delimiter-token]] that separates the parts of an input. [[fig-1]] draws this head and the language modelling head sitting on the same stack. Which token sequence gets fed in is a separate question, handled in the next chapter over.", "childId": null, "number": "6.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "what-l2-moves", "title": "What the Label Loss Actually Moves", "body": "[[eq-supervised-objective]] has the same shape as the pre-training loss. Both are [[maximum-likelihood-training]]: sum a log-probability the model assigns to something observed.\n\nThe difference is what is being summed over. $L_1$ runs over token positions and scores a distribution over the vocabulary. [[supervised-objective-l2]] runs over instances in [[labeled-dataset-c]] and scores a distribution over that task's labels.\n\nWrite it that way and the only thing separating one task from another is which $\\mathcal{C}$ you plug in.\n\nNow the part the equation leaves implicit. Nothing in $L_2$ restricts the gradient to $W_y$. The loss is differentiated through the whole stack, so every pre-trained weight moves — embeddings, attention projections, feed-forward layers, all twelve blocks.\n\nThat is worth separating from the parameter-count claim. \"Only $W_y$ is added\" is a statement about what has to be freshly initialized. It is not a claim that the rest is frozen. Each fine-tuned task ends up owning a full, separately modified copy of the stack.\n\nThis is what distinguishes the recipe from [[feature-based-transfer]]. There the pre-trained model is held fixed and its hidden states are fed as features into a task network trained from scratch, as in [[elmo-deep-contextualized-word-representations]]. The [[per-task-parameter-cost]] in that setup is a whole new model per task. Here it is one matrix, bought at the price of updating everything behind it.\n\nWhich raises the obvious worry. A twelve-block stack is being fitted on [[rte]]'s 2,490 training examples, or [[sts-b]]'s roughly 5,700.\n\nThe paper does not confront overfitting directly. It adds a second loss.", "childId": null, "number": "6.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]}}, {"id": "why-the-old-loss-stays-on", "title": "Why the Old Loss Stays On", "body": "Look closely at the argument in [[eq-combined-objective]]. The auxiliary term is $L_1(\\mathcal{C})$, not $L_1(U)$.\n\nSo the next-token loss is no longer computed on [[bookscorpus]]. It runs on the task's own labelled inputs, read simply as text. A premise, a delimiter and a hypothesis get scored token by token as if they were a passage from a book, at the same time as they are scored for their label.\n\nThe machinery for that is already present. The language modelling head is the transposed token embedding matrix ([[output-softmax-tied-embeddings]], reusing [[token-embedding-matrix]]), so keeping [[auxiliary-lm-objective]] switched on costs no new parameters at all. A model with a separately trained output projection would have had to carry one along on purpose.\n\n[[auxiliary-objective-weight]] sets the balance at $0.5$. The paper reports the value in its setup section, and does not report searching over it or testing how sensitive results are to it.\n\nThe two stated benefits — better generalization, faster convergence — are given as findings, not as consequences of an argument. No mechanism is offered. Prior work is cited as having seen the same thing.\n\nThen the paper's own ablation complicates it. Removing the term ([[ablation-no-auxiliary-objective]] in [[table-5]]) raises the unweighted average by 0.3 points. It helps on the inference tasks and on [[qqp]], and hurts on the smaller sets, which the paper reads as a size trend.\n\nSo the default configuration carries a term whose average measured effect, in this paper, is slightly negative.\n\nOne more thing has happened here. [[auxiliary-training-objectives]] appeared in the related work as a rival branch, the transfer mechanism in its own right. Here the same device is demoted to a supplement sitting on top of pre-training.", "childId": "n-from-language-model-to-classifier--why-the-old-loss-stays-on", "number": "6.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "three-passes-quarter-rate", "title": "Three Passes at a Quarter of the Rate", "body": "[[fine-tuning-hyperparameters]] states a default: unless the paper says otherwise, stage two keeps stage one's settings. [[adam-optimizer-gpt1]] stays, [[regularization-setup]] stays, the vocabulary stays.\n\nThe exceptions are few, and each is a step down in scale.\n\nThe rate is $6.25\\text{e-}5$ ([[fine-tuning-lr-and-batch]]). Divide [[pre-training-optimization]]'s peak of $2.5\\text{e-}4$ by it and you get exactly 4. Batch size drops from 64 sequences to 32. Since a pre-training batch was 64 contiguous sequences of 512 tokens ([[pretraining-schedule]]), about 32,768 tokens, a fine-tuning batch is smaller in tokens too, by however short the task inputs happen to be.\n\nThe schedule changes shape as well. Pre-training annealed on a cosine curve ([[cosine-annealing]]); [[fine-tuning-lr-schedule]] decays linearly to zero.\n\nAnd the warmup is now relative. Pre-training warmed up over a fixed 2,000 updates ([[linear-warmup]]); fine-tuning warms up over the first 0.2% of training.\n\nWalk that through. [[snli]] has around 550,000 training examples, so [[three-epochs-fine-tuning]] at batch 32 is about 51,500 updates, and 0.2% of that is roughly 103 updates of warmup. [[rte]] has 2,490 examples: about 233 updates in total, and 0.2% of that is under half of one. The same rule gives a real warmup on the largest set and none at all on the smallest. The paper does not comment on this.\n\nThe only addition is [[classifier-dropout]] at 0.1 on the new head.\n\nRead against [[ulmfit]]'s staged schedule, the point of this list is what is missing from it. No layer-wise rates, no unfreezing order. Three passes, one rate, decay to zero — and, the paper hedges, that sufficed for most datasets.", "childId": null, "number": "6.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]}}], "number": "6", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-one-sequence-per-task": {"id": "n-one-sequence-per-task", "parentId": "root", "parentChapterId": "one-sequence-per-task", "depth": 1, "title": "One Sequence Per Task", "intro": "The parent said where the paper puts the task's structure: in the input, not the architecture. This level says why that is the only place it can go given what sits on top of the stack, what each of the four input shapes costs to run, and what the parameter claim does and does not cover.", "chapters": [{"id": "where-the-difference-goes", "title": "What the Other Option Actually Was", "body": "Start with the alternative, drawn concretely.\n\nThe systems this paper measures itself against are mostly task-built recurrent networks ([[baseline-comparisons]]). For an entailment dataset that typically means a biLSTM reading each sentence, an attention mechanism aligning one against the other, and a classifier on the aligned result. Under [[feature-based-transfer]] — [[elmo-deep-contextualized-word-representations]] is the named case — the transferred part is a set of contextual vectors, and that pairing network is built on top of them.\n\nTwo things follow from that arrangement.\n\nThe pairing network starts from random weights. And it learns from exactly one source: the target task's labelled examples.\n\nSo the component that does the structural work — the part that actually knows there are two sentences and has to relate them — is the component trained on the least data. That is the sharp form of the paper's objection. Counted in parameters rather than in data, the same objection is the [[per-task-parameter-cost]] complaint, and the scarcity it runs into is the one the paper opened on ([[labeled-data-scarcity]]).\n\nThe paper's replacement is credited to earlier work, not claimed as its own: it cites the [[traversal-style-approach]] and applies it.\n\nBut the swap has a price, and the paper does not dwell on it. [[structured-inputs]] arrive with their parts already separated and named. After the transformation, the only surviving trace of that structure is token order plus one symbol. Whatever the pairing network was computing explicitly, the stack must now compute implicitly, using weights trained only to predict the next word. The paper does not argue that this suffices. It reports scores for [[task-agnostic-model]] instead, and lets them carry the point.", "childId": null, "number": "7.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "one-vector-at-one-position", "title": "One Vector, at One Position", "body": "The transformations look like a stylistic choice until you look at what consumes their output.\n\n[[eq-task-head]] is the whole task-specific classifier. Take the final block's activation at the last input token ([[final-token-activation]]), multiply by one matrix ([[linear-output-layer-wy]]), softmax.\n\nNotice what the head never receives. It does not read the sequence. It reads a single vector, at a single position.\n\nAnd because attention is masked ([[masked-self-attention]]), that position is the only one that could serve. Every earlier token was forbidden from attending rightward, so only the last position has seen the whole input.\n\nThe format therefore has one hard requirement: that last position must exist and must mean the same thing in every example. This is the job of [[start-and-end-tokens]]. Each transformed input is wrapped, so the vector the classifier reads is always the activation over $\\langle e\\rangle$ — never over whatever content word happened to land last, which would vary example to example.\n\nThe [[delimiter-token]] handles the complementary job inside the sequence, marking where one part stops and the next starts.\n\nBoth symbols are new. Neither occurred during pre-training, so their embeddings are initialized randomly and fitted during fine-tuning alone — three passes over the task's labelled set, at a learning rate of $6.25\\times10^{-5}$ ([[fine-tuning-hyperparameters]]).\n\nThat is a thin budget for symbols that have to come to mean \"a boundary happens here\" and \"the input ends here\". The paper reports the recipe works; it never isolates this piece. No ablation removes the brackets or the delimiter, so how much of the result rests on them is untested.\n\nOne small ambiguity: the delimiter is written `$`, a character books already contain, yet delimiter embeddings are counted among the parameters a new task adds. So it is being treated as its own entry in the [[byte-pair-encoding-gpt1]] vocabulary, not as the ordinary currency symbol reused.", "childId": null, "number": "7.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}], "pages": [3, 4, 5]}}, {"id": "the-four-shapes", "title": "Four Shapes, and What Each Costs to Run", "body": "The four templates are not equally cheap, and the differences are worth naming.\n\n[[text-classification]] needs no transformation at all. A single sentence already is one ordered run of tokens, so those datasets are fine-tuned directly.\n\nEntailment adds one symbol and nothing else: premise, delimiter, hypothesis, one forward pass ([[entailment-input-transformation]]). The task hands over the order, so there is no decision to make.\n\nSimilarity is where the readout bites. The stack runs left to right and summarizes at the end, so whichever sentence is placed second sits nearer the summary, while the first has to survive the whole second sentence to reach it. Picking an order would make the prediction depend on an arbitrary choice the task never made.\n\nSo both orderings are built, each is run through the stack on its own, and the two final activations are added before the linear layer ([[similarity-input-transformation]]). Element-wise addition is symmetric, so swapping which sentence you called first leaves the head's input unchanged. The fix costs two forward passes per example.\n\nQuestion answering has a different problem: the number of candidates varies, and a fixed architecture cannot have a variable number of output slots. Building $[z; q; \\$; a_k]$ once per candidate sidesteps that by running the same fixed model several times ([[qa-and-commonsense-input-transformation]]). The softmax then normalizes across sequences rather than across label columns inside one sequence. The paper does not state the head's shape here, but for a distribution over candidates to come out, each pass must yield a single score.\n\nCost scales with the option count: two passes for the two endings of the [[story-cloze-test]], more for a multi-option exam question ([[race-dataset]]).\n\nOne limit goes unmentioned. The stack reads at most 512 tokens ([[context-window-512-tokens]]), while a comprehension sequence carries a full passage plus a question plus an answer. The paper says nothing about what happens when that overflows.\n\n[[fig-1]] draws all four beside the [[decoder-only-stack]] they feed.", "childId": "n-one-sequence-per-task--the-four-shapes", "number": "7.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "what-a-new-task-adds", "title": "Counting What a New Task Adds", "body": "The parameter claim ([[minimal-extra-parameters]]) is checkable, so check it.\n\n$W_y$ maps one 768-dimensional activation ([[hidden-state-dimension-768]]) to label scores. Three-way inference needs $768 \\times 3$, which is $2{,}304$ weights. Binary sentiment needs $1{,}536$. Multiple choice, producing one score per sequence, needs $768$. A delimiter embedding is another $768$ numbers.\n\nNow the denominator. From [[model-specification]], each block's self-attention holds four $768 \\times 768$ matrices; $768 \\times 768$ is $589{,}824$, so that sublayer is about $2.4$ million weights. The feed-forward sublayer holds two matrices of $768 \\times 3072$, which is $2{,}359{,}296$ each, so about $4.7$ million more. Call it $7$ million per block. With [[twelve-layer-depth]], the stack alone is roughly $85$ million, before the token embedding table is counted.\n\nThe arithmetic is mine from the stated dimensions; the paper reports no total.\n\nSo the comparison is a couple of thousand new numbers against tens of millions of transferred ones. The claim survives.\n\nBut read what it counts. These are the parameters that start from random initialization — not the parameters that change. The supervised loss ([[eq-supervised-objective]]) backpropagates through every block, so fine-tuning moves the entire stack.\n\nThat means each of the twelve datasets ends with its own complete copy of the weights. Nothing here is shared at deployment time.\n\nThe claim is about how much untrained machinery a task requires, not about storage. Which is the right claim for the argument it answers, since the objection to the alternative was precisely that its new components learn from scratch on scarce labels.", "childId": null, "number": "7.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "format-decides-the-menu", "title": "Four Templates, Twelve Datasets", "body": "Line the templates up against the evaluation menu ([[evaluation-task-families]], [[table-1]]) and the ratio is the point.\n\n[[natural-language-inference]] supplies five datasets, all of them the premise-delimiter-hypothesis shape. [[semantic-similarity]] supplies three, all on the two-pass symmetric shape. [[question-answering-and-commonsense-reasoning]] supplies two, both on the one-sequence-per-candidate shape. Classification supplies two, on no shape at all.\n\nFive plus three plus two plus two is twelve.\n\nSo breadth of tasks is not breadth of formats. Twelve datasets are covered by four templates, one of which is the empty template. Several of the datasets come bundled in [[glue-benchmark]], which is part of why the menu could be assembled this quickly.\n\nThe sizes are not uniform either. They run from roughly $5{,}700$ training examples on [[sts-b]] up to roughly $550{,}000$ on [[snli]], a spread of about two orders of magnitude, and the paper uses that spread to argue the recipe is not only a large-data technique.\n\nOne thing the menu holds constant is worth naming, because the transformations depend on it. Every one of the twelve ends in a label from a fixed set, or a choice among candidates the dataset supplies. None asks for generated text.\n\nThat is what lets the whole task-side apparatus be a serializer plus one scoring matrix. A task whose output is itself structured would need something else, and the paper does not claim it.\n\n[[etheme-one-stack-any-input-shape|One stack, any input shape]] — with the shapes counted, and the outputs kept simple.", "childId": null, "number": "7.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "7", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-the-scoreboard": {"id": "n-the-scoreboard", "parentId": "root", "parentChapterId": "the-scoreboard", "depth": 1, "title": "Reading the Scoreboard", "intro": "The parent gave the margins. This level gives the numbers on both sides of them, the units each column is measured in, and the three rows where the model does not come first.", "chapters": [{"id": "reading-the-columns", "title": "What a Point Means, and Who Is on the Other Side", "body": "Before any margin means anything, two things about the tables need saying.\n\nThe first is that a \"point\" is not one currency. [[absolute-improvement|Gains in percentage points]] are quoted across every task, but the columns of [[table-4]] are not all measuring the same quantity. Its header spells this out: [[cola|CoLA]] is scored by Matthews correlation, [[sst-2|SST-2]] by accuracy, [[mrpc|MRPC]] and [[qqp|QQP]] by F1, [[sts-b|STS-B]] by Pearson correlation.\n\nMatthews correlation is computed from the confusion matrix of a binary classifier and runs from $-1$ to $+1$, with $0$ at chance, reported here multiplied by 100. F1 is the harmonic mean of precision and recall on the positive class. Pearson correlation compares the model's graded similarity scores against the human ones.\n\nSo a 10.4 move on CoLA and a 4.2 move on QQP are moves along different scales. Adding them together is exactly what an aggregate score does, and the tables do it.\n\nThe second is what the numbers are being beaten. The [[baseline-comparisons|previous bests]] are not one system but a shelf of them, each built for its own dataset, and mostly recurrent — a [[lstm-gpt1|biLSTM]] core with attention, ELMo features, or both.\n\nSeveral are ensembles. [[table-2|Table 2]] annotates rows with `5x` and `3x` to mark ensembles of five and three models, and marks the previous best in each column by underlining it.\n\nThe paper's own entry is one row in each table, and it is a single model everywhere. That is the comparison being set up: one set of fine-tuned weights against a per-dataset field, some of which is voting.", "childId": null, "number": "8.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "the-margins", "title": "The Numbers Behind the Margins", "body": "With the units fixed, the head-to-head figures are worth reading directly.\n\nOn [[natural-language-inference|inference]], [[multinli|MultiNLI]] is reported in two columns, matched and mismatched: 82.1 and 81.4, against 80.6 and 80.1 from a three-model ensemble. The 1.5 the paper quotes is the matched column; the mismatched gain is 1.3.\n\n[[snli|SNLI]] moves from 89.3 to 89.9. The 89.3 was posted by two separate five-model ensembles, so the 0.6 is small but taken off a crowded ceiling.\n\n[[scitail|SciTail]] goes 83.3 to 88.3 and [[qnli|QNLI]] 82.3 to 88.1 — five and 5.8 points, both against single systems rather than ensembles.\n\nThe [[question-answering-and-commonsense-reasoning|reading and reasoning]] numbers in [[table-3]] have more inside them than the headline. [[race-dataset|RACE]] is split into middle-school and high-school questions. Middle school goes 60.2 to 62.9, a gain of 2.7. High school goes 50.3 to 57.4, a gain of 7.1.\n\nThe 5.7 overall is a blend of those two, and it is the harder split that carries it. The system passed on both is a nine-model ensemble.\n\nNote also the absolute level: 59.0 overall on RACE. The margin is wide, but it sits low on the scale, on a dataset picked because it holds more reasoning-type questions than the [[cnn-and-squad-contrast|extraction-style alternatives]].\n\n[[story-cloze-test|Story Cloze]] is the widest gap on any single number, 77.6 to 86.5. The paper reads the pair as evidence about long-range context, which is an interpretation laid over the numbers, not something the numbers separate out on their own.", "childId": "n-the-scoreboard--the-margins", "number": "8.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "the-three-it-loses", "title": "The Three Rows It Does Not Win", "body": "Nine of twelve leaves three, and the tables name them.\n\n[[rte|RTE]] is the one the paper discusses: 56.0 against 61.7 from a multi-task biLSTM. Its explanation is dataset size, 2,490 training examples, and its proposed fix is multi-task training, which it states it did not run.\n\nThe other two are quieter. On [[mrpc]] the model scores 82.3 F1 against a previous best of 86.0 — and the multi-task biLSTM in the same table scores 83.5, so this is third place, not second. On SST-2 the same pattern: 91.3 against 93.2 from the best system, with the multi-task biLSTM at 91.6 in between.\n\nSo one loss falls in [[semantic-similarity|similarity]] and one in [[text-classification|classification]], and neither is explained.\n\nThe size story does not hold on its own either. STS-B has roughly 5,700 training examples, more than twice RTE's but still the smallest set in the [[evaluation-task-families|evaluation]], and it is a win. Size alone does not sort the wins from the losses.\n\nThere is one more thing visible in the same table that the paper does not take up. The multi-task biLSTM that beats it on RTE scores 18.9 on CoLA, where the single-task version of the same architecture scores 35.0.\n\nMulti-task training, in the paper's own comparison set, helps on some columns and costs heavily on others. That makes the suggested fix for RTE less obviously free than the sentence proposing it implies, and it remains untested here either way — one of the softer spots in the [[state-of-the-art-9-of-12|nine of twelve]] claim.", "childId": null, "number": "8.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "adding-up", "title": "The Aggregate, and Where the Count Comes From", "body": "Two summary numbers close the evaluation, and both are assembled rather than measured.\n\nThe [[glue-benchmark|GLUE]] score of 72.8 against 68.9 is an aggregate over the tasks in its column, so a large move anywhere inside it lifts the total. The single-task biLSTM with ELMo and attention scores 64.8 on the same aggregate; the multi-task version, 68.9.\n\nThe column that moves furthest inside that aggregate is CoLA, and CoLA is also the column whose metric is least like an accuracy. The paper reads its 45.4 as evidence of linguistic bias picked up in pre-training. That reading is plausible and the number does not settle it.\n\nThe headline count is assembled the same way. Nine of twelve appears in no table. You get it by reading three of them and counting the bold rows: four inference wins, two reading wins, [[qqp]], STS-B and CoLA — with [[table-1]] fixing the denominator at twelve datasets across four families.\n\nWhat the whole board does establish is narrow and real. One pre-trained stack, fine-tuned per task with a task-shaped input and a linear head as in [[fig-1]], beats per-task systems on most rows and several ensembles among them.\n\nWhat it does not establish is which part of the recipe earned that. The scoreboard measures the finished pipeline end to end, and separating the pre-training, the architecture and the auxiliary objective takes a different experiment.\n\n[[theme-what-the-twelve-datasets-show|The twelve datasets in full]]; [[etheme-the-scoreboard-and-its-gaps|the gaps in the board]].", "childId": null, "number": "8.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "8", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, "n-which-parts-do-the-work": {"id": "n-which-parts-do-the-work", "parentId": "root", "parentChapterId": "which-parts-do-the-work", "depth": 1, "title": "Which Parts Do the Work, and What Is Left Open", "intro": "The parent named the three diagnostics. This level reads them as experiments: what was actually varied and what was held fixed, how each panel of Figure 2 is drawn and what its axes hide, which datasets the ablation table covers, and where the paper's own numbers disagree with each other.", "chapters": [{"id": "layer-sweep", "title": "Thirteen Runs Up the Stack", "body": "The left panel of [[fig-2]] is not one training run. It is a series of them, one per point.\n\nThe x-axis counts layers transferred, from 0 to 13. That is one slot for the embedding layer plus one for each block in the [[twelve-layer-depth]] stack. At position 1 only the [[token-embedding-matrix]] and the [[learned-position-embeddings]] carry over from [[generative-pretraining]]; every transformer block is initialized fresh. At 13 the whole pre-trained network carries over. Each position gets its own [[supervised-fine-tuning]] run.\n\nSo the leftmost useful point is roughly the older practice of [[word-embedding-transfer]], and the sweep asks what the blocks add on top of it.\n\nTwo datasets are plotted, and the axes are not shared. [[race-dataset]] dev accuracy reads off a left axis spanning 30 to 100, rising from about 39.7 to about 60. [[multinli]] dev accuracy reads off a right axis spanning 70 to 100, rising from about 72.5 to about 82.\n\nThat second window is a third as tall over the same plot height. A MultiNLI point therefore looks about two and a half times steeper than a RACE point of the same size. Read the colour before the slope.\n\nDashed lines are training accuracy from the same runs. They sit above the dev lines and climb further, so extra transferred layers also fit the labelled data harder.\n\nWhat the panel licenses is the monotone total. What it does not measure is any individual layer: the sweep only ever adds blocks from the bottom up, so it never asks what layers 6 through 12 are worth on their own. The reading in [[layers-transferred]] is consistent with the curve rather than demonstrated by it, on two datasets, on dev sets, one run each.", "childId": null, "number": "9.1", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "scoring-not-labelling", "title": "Turning a Next-Token Model Into a Scorer", "body": "The pre-trained model emits one thing: a distribution over the next token, formed by [[output-softmax-tied-embeddings]]. Nothing in it names a class.\n\nTo compare two candidate answers you can score each string by the summed log-probability of its tokens. That comparison is broken by length. Every token contributes a negative number, so the shorter candidate wins by default.\n\nDivide by the token count and the bias goes away. That mean is [[average-token-log-probability-scoring]], and three of the four constructions in [[zero-shot-behaviors]] rest on it.\n\nOn RACE the model is conditioned on the document and the question, each candidate answer is scored, and the highest wins ([[race-zero-shot-heuristic]]). This is the fine-tuning-time [[qa-and-commonsense-input-transformation]] with the classifier removed.\n\nDPRD is a set of Winograd schemas: a definite pronoun with two possible antecedents ([[coreference]]). The heuristic substitutes each antecedent for the pronoun and scores the rest of the sequence after the substitution ([[dprd-winograd-heuristic]]). Both readings score the same trailing tokens, so length is matched exactly and only the conditioning context differs.\n\n[[cola-zero-shot-heuristic]] is the odd one of the three. Acceptability is a single-sentence judgement with no competing candidate, so the score is compared against a threshold instead. The paper does not say how that threshold is set, which makes it a free parameter fitted somewhere outside the described procedure, unlike the three argmax constructions.\n\nSentiment uses a different device entirely ([[sst2-zero-shot-heuristic]]). Appending *very* and reading off the relative probability of *positive* against *negative* consults one next-token distribution, not a whole-string score. It also assumes the corpus phrases sentiment that way. The paper calls all four heuristic solutions and does not argue they are the best available ones for [[cola]] or [[sst-2]].", "childId": null, "number": "9.2", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "curves-over-training", "title": "Watching the Heuristics Climb", "body": "The right panel of [[fig-2]] shares nothing with the left one but the page. Its x-axis is pre-training updates on a log scale, $10^3$ to $10^6$, which is the run described in [[pre-training-optimization]] sampled as it went.\n\nThe y-axis is rescaled per task. Zero is a random-guess baseline and one is the best published single-model result on that task. A value of 0.68 means the heuristic closed 68% of that gap.\n\nThat rescaling is what lets four differently-scored tasks share an axis. It also means the ceiling is a moving reference from the [[baseline-comparisons]] of the day, and that no absolute accuracy can be read off the plot at all.\n\nAll four solid curves rise and largely stay risen. Nothing in the run is optimizing them: the only loss being minimized is [[language-modeling-objective]], and the corpus is books. The behaviours arrive as a side effect of fitting it, which the [[token-level-perplexity]] of 18.4 says was fitted well.\n\nOne alternative the panel does not separate: these heuristics all read fluency, and fluency improves with perplexity. A curve that rises with better next-token prediction is what you would see either way.\n\nThe dashed lines are an LSTM language model trained the same way. They reach lower and move around more, and the sentiment curve drops from 0.42 to 0.30 before recovering.\n\nThe paper offers this as support for [[transformer-inductive-bias]] — that attention over a wide context, restricted by [[masked-self-attention]], is a more structured memory for [[long-range-dependencies-gpt1]] than a recurrent state. It is one [[lstm-gpt1]] configuration against one transformer, single runs, with variance judged by eye off a plot. The paper writes *suggesting*, and that is the right strength for it.", "childId": null, "number": "9.3", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "three-removals", "title": "Three Removals, Eight Datasets", "body": "[[table-5]] is four recipes down the side and eight datasets across the top. Those eight are the [[glue-benchmark]] tasks.\n\nRACE and the [[story-cloze-test]] are not among them. The two datasets carrying the paper's largest margins are therefore absent from every row of its [[ablation-studies]].\n\nThe last column averages the eight numbers, unweighted. Those numbers are Matthews correlation, accuracy, F1 and Pearson correlation, so the average adds a correlation point to an accuracy point as if they were the same unit.\n\nThe full system scores 74.7. Removing pre-training gives 59.9, and $74.7 - 59.9 = 14.8$, with every column down ([[ablation-no-pretraining]]). One column dominates: CoLA falls from 45.4 to 18.9, and that 26.5-point drop divided across eight datasets contributes 3.3 of the 14.8 on its own.\n\nThe architecture swap gives 69.1, so $74.7 - 69.1 = 5.6$ ([[ablation-lstm-substitution]]). The substituted model is a single-layer LSTM of 2048 units, against twelve blocks of [[hidden-state-dimension-768]]. Depth, width and architecture all change together, so the 5.6 is not attributable to recurrence alone. The LSTM takes one column, [[mrpc]], at 83.2 against 82.3.\n\nThe third row is the one that does not behave. Fine-tuning with the supervised loss alone scores 75.0, three tenths above the full model, and takes the best cell in five of the eight columns ([[ablation-no-auxiliary-objective]]).\n\n[[auxiliary-lm-objective]] earns its keep on exactly three: [[qqp]], [[multinli|MNLI]] and QNLI. Those are the larger sets, which is the trend the paper states and the most it claims.\n\nThe recipe keeps a component that its own table scores slightly against on average, with [[auxiliary-objective-weight]] fixed at 0.5 and never swept.", "childId": "n-which-parts-do-the-work--three-removals", "number": "9.4", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}, {"id": "numbers-that-disagree", "title": "The Numbers That Do Not Line Up", "body": "[[rte]] is the clearest loss. The model reaches 56.0 against 61.7 from a multi-task biLSTM in [[table-2]], 5.7 points short, on 2,490 training examples. The paper suggests multi-task training would help and reports no such run. RTE is also one of the eight ablation columns, so the smallest set in the evaluation carries an eighth of every average in Table 5.\n\nThe GLUE figure moves between sections. [[table-4]]'s last column reads 68.9 to 72.8, and $72.8 - 68.9 = 3.9$. The introduction's headline for the same benchmark is 5.5. The paper does not reconcile the two, and the GLUE column folds in inference datasets that Table 4 does not show.\n\nSeveral wins are small enough that the missing variance matters. SNLI gains 0.6 in Table 2. [[sts-b]] gains 1 point in Table 4. Every number on both sides is a single reported run against a single published best, with no repeats and no spread given.\n\n[[state-of-the-art-9-of-12]] counts wins across [[evaluation-task-families]] without weighting them, so a 0.6 and the 8.9 in [[table-3]] each contribute one. The three that are not wins are RTE, SST-2 and [[mrpc]]. The gains are stated as [[absolute-improvement]] throughout, which keeps the arithmetic checkable.\n\nThe conclusion adds a claim the experiments do not carry. It says the model acquires significant [[world-knowledge]] from predicting long stretches of text. Every task in the evaluation rewards reading the passage in front of the model, and nothing in the design separates knowledge brought to a passage from comprehension of it.", "childId": null, "number": "9.5", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}], "number": "9", "sources": {"sections": [{"id": "abstract", "title": "Abstract", "start": 1, "end": 1}, {"id": "1", "title": "Introduction", "start": 1, "end": 2}, {"id": "2", "title": "Related Work", "start": 2, "end": 3}, {"id": "3", "title": "Framework", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.1", "title": "Unsupervised pre-training", "start": 3, "end": 3}, {"id": "3.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 3, "end": 4}, {"id": "3.3", "title": "Task-specific input transformations", "start": 4, "end": 4}, {"id": "4.1", "title": "Setup", "start": 4, "end": 5}, {"id": "4.2", "title": "Supervised fine-tuning", "start": 5, "end": 7}, {"id": "5", "title": "Analysis", "start": 7, "end": 8}, {"id": "6", "title": "Conclusion", "start": 8, "end": 8}], "pages": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]}}});
